WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.568
The biggest bank collapse

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.870
since the 2008 financial crisis.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.105
And it shut down today.

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:06.473
It's called the Silicon Valley Bank,

00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:08.008
and it is one of the tech industry's

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:10.143
largest lenders also lend to individuals

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:11.011
across the country.

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:12.145
It is now under control

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:13.413
of the federal government.

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:14.614
It was a sudden implosion

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:16.850
and it came after a run on deposits.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.418
So the bank stock lost

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:19.919
80% of its value this week.

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:21.788
60% in one day alone.

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:24.124
Yesterday, this morning, shares halted.

00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:25.725
It was over quickly.

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:26.760
And then police showed up

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.560
at the bank headquarters

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:28.528
in California today.

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:30.263
You're looking at that here

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:31.498
Customers reportedly

00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:32.298
even lined up at a bank

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:34.434
branch on Park Avenue in New York City.

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:36.002
The NYPD showed up there.

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:36.870
Those customers

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.972
demanding their deposits back.

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:39.973
Now to be clear,

00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:40.707
Silicon Valley

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:42.542
Bank is a huge bank and its customers

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:44.677
include individuals as well as household

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:46.613
names, you may know, like Airbnb,

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:48.248
Pinterest, Cisco.

00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:50.383
And this is a way bigger story.

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:51.484
The entire banking

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:53.186
industry is feeling the impact.

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:54.187
US banks at one

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:55.121
point had lost over

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:57.457
$100 billion in stock market value

00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:58.591
in just two days.

00:00:58.591 --> 00:00:59.459
And there are fears

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:01.761
now that this could spread beyond banking

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:03.329
to other parts of the economy.

00:01:05.265 --> 00:01:06.733
This is like the SNL

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.268
crisis in the eighties.

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:08.835
We're not talking

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:10.070
about a failure of this bank,

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.839
but the banks could, you know, fail.

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:15.642
Not all of this is clearly an example

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:16.843
of something breaking.

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:18.445
We can assume that there are serious

00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:20.346
contagion here.

00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:21.247
Okay.

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:22.615
So when you use the word contagion,

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:23.850
it begs the question,

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.919
why could this happen at other banks?

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:27.353
Well, for one basic reason,

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:28.688
Silicon Valley Bank was doing

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:29.556
what a lot of other

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:31.224
banks are doing right now

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:33.226
slip to customer deposits.

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:33.893
And they went

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:35.728
and invested that money in other things

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:37.831
like U.S. government debt and mortgages.

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:38.665
And they've been doing that

00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:39.799
for quite a while.

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:40.333
Well,

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:41.668
when the Fed started to hike rates

00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:43.236
aggressively, that meant that Silicon

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:43.770
Valley bank

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:44.737
immediately lost money

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:45.905
on those investments

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:47.040
at the very same time

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.775
as they were losing money

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:49.976
as interest rates rose.

00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:51.978
Those bank customers, those depositors,

00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:53.713
they needed to withdraw more money.

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:55.715
So they started to sell assets,

00:01:55.715 --> 00:01:56.349
losing money

00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:57.650
just to raise the money,

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.185
needed to make good on deposit

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:00.453
withdrawal requests.

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:01.588
And then that turned

00:02:01.588 --> 00:02:02.655
into a spiral of fear

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:03.723
and a race among customers

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:05.792
to withdraw assets. The run on the bank.

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:07.327
And yesterday afternoon,

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:09.095
the CEO of Silicon Valley Bank,

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:10.497
his name is Greg Becker,

00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:11.731
hosted a call with clients

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:12.332
and said, quote,

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:14.000
My ask is to stay calm

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.535
because that's what's important.

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:17.637
We have been long term supporters of you.

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:19.072
The last thing we need you to do

00:02:19.072 --> 00:02:20.507
is panic.

00:02:20.507 --> 00:02:22.775
Well, when that's the ask as a bank,

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:24.043
you're toast.

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:24.811
And one person

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:26.012
familiar with the situation

00:02:26.012 --> 00:02:26.446
asking me

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:28.148
the hypothetical question tonight

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:28.815
what prevents

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:29.716
runs on a whole bunch

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:32.051
more banks next week? Unclear.

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:33.753
It is an important moment.

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:34.154
And tonight,

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:35.755
the deputy treasury secretary,

00:02:35.755 --> 00:02:38.024
Wally Adeyemo, is trying to calm fears.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:39.626
In an exclusive interview with CNN,

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:42.095
federal

00:02:42.095 --> 00:02:43.463
regulators are paying

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:44.597
attention to this

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:47.000
particular financial institution and that

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:48.568
when we think about the broader

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:50.503
financial system,

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:52.405
we're very confident in the ability

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:55.341
and the resilience of the system.

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:56.643
Alison Greenberg is OUTFRONT.

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:58.111
She is the CEO of Ruth Health.

00:02:58.111 --> 00:02:59.546
It's a maternity care startup

00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:01.481
that has money deposited in Silicon

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:02.749
Valley Bank.

00:03:02.782 --> 00:03:03.283
So, Alison,

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:04.651
thank you very much for being with me.

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:07.287
So you are right in the middle of this.

00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:08.721
Let's just start with.

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:10.323
When you realized you

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:12.392
a startup, you've got money at this bank.

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:14.127
You're using that money to make payroll

00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:15.361
and other things.

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:16.563
When did you find out

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:18.164
something was wrong?

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:20.433
So my co-founder, Audrey Wu,

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:21.768
and I received an email

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:24.370
from one of our seed investors.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.239
The email seemed cryptic.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.441
It was strange. It was urgent.

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:31.177
It was not like the other email he sends

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:33.947
And we were in meetings.

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:34.948
You know, we run a busy

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:36.449
maternal health startup.

00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:38.451
We have so many concerns every day

00:03:38.451 --> 00:03:40.987
that this email just didn't make sense.

00:03:41.588 --> 00:03:42.922
So a few hours later,

00:03:42.922 --> 00:03:44.524
I called the investor and I said, Hey,

00:03:44.524 --> 00:03:46.526
what was this email about?

00:03:46.526 --> 00:03:47.927
He was out of breath,

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:49.729
like he had just run a marathon.

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:52.632
And he said, Take your money out of SBB,

00:03:52.632 --> 00:03:53.766
go into your account,

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:56.002
take your money out as soon as possible.

00:03:56.703 --> 00:03:58.871
And, you know, as a business owner,

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:00.273
my co-founder,

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:00.540
Adrian

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.408
I constantly think about two things.

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:04.010
We think about our team,

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:06.179
the people we employ and their families.

00:04:06.179 --> 00:04:07.847
And we think about our patients,

00:04:07.847 --> 00:04:09.349
the mothers, the new moms

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:10.750
and the families that we support

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.118
and their families.

00:04:12.118 --> 00:04:15.655
And so for us, it was do this now

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:17.257
or else. Okay.

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:18.524
And this was what, yesterday

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:19.592
or the day before?

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:20.526
This was yesterday

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:22.829
between the hours of noon and 3 p.m..

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:23.997
Yesterday between the hours of noon

00:04:23.997 --> 00:04:24.430
and 3 p.m.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:24.831
you get this.

00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:27.300
So then you do it. You go and you.

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:28.868
And how does that happen?

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:29.936
They actually give you the money

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:31.070
I mean, these are the 3 hours

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:32.839
before the world fell apart.

00:04:32.839 --> 00:04:34.807
So what happened then?

00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:35.008
Well,

00:04:35.008 --> 00:04:36.242
we were incredibly lucky

00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:38.311
to have that news early from an investor.

00:04:39.178 --> 00:04:41.047
We didn't withdraw the money immediately.

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:41.247
You know,

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:42.782
we're conscientious business owners.

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:44.317
We spoke to a few other investors.

00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:45.752
We spoke to friends.

00:04:45.752 --> 00:04:46.352
I mean,

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:47.887
this is like calls going off the hook

00:04:47.887 --> 00:04:49.922
for the last 36 hours.

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:51.357
When we felt confident

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:53.793
that this was going to be a crisis.

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:55.461
Audrey acted fast.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:56.963
And this is the kind of fear

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:58.298
that actually incites focus.

00:04:58.298 --> 00:04:59.365
We withdrew the money

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:01.134
through transfers and wires

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:02.535
as soon as possible.

00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:03.736
I mean, she was withdrawing

00:05:03.736 --> 00:05:04.470
small amounts,

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.071
big amounts

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:06.572
just to not set off an eye

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:08.641
that might limit our withdrawals.

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:10.243
And we were able to remove

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:11.511
this was not our only bank account,

00:05:11.511 --> 00:05:12.078
but we were able

00:05:12.078 --> 00:05:14.447
to remove the majority of the funds

00:05:14.447 --> 00:05:15.948
so that what was left

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:17.917
when the website crashed

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:20.353
was below the $250,000.

00:05:20.553 --> 00:05:21.888
Right. So you have money there.

00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:24.090
And I know the FDIC in terms of

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:24.791
because the amount

00:05:24.791 --> 00:05:25.258
I mean, obviously

00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:26.159
had a lot more than that there.

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:27.493
You were able to get that out.

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:28.394
The amount that you have

00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:29.162
that's insured now

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:31.130
by the FDIC, the 250,000.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:31.798
Your understanding

00:05:31.798 --> 00:05:32.432
is you're going to get that

00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:34.267
pretty much right away.

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:36.202
What we're hearing is by Monday. Okay.

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:36.903
So that that

00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:38.004
I think that should give people calm.

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:38.705
The FDIC

00:05:38.705 --> 00:05:40.006
if you're under the limit

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:41.140
it gives you

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:43.209
is a holding is holding strong on this.

00:05:43.643 --> 00:05:44.911
So Barron's is reporting

00:05:44.911 --> 00:05:45.912
that the CEO Greg Becker

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:46.612
I just mentioned him

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:47.647
I know you weren't on that call,

00:05:47.647 --> 00:05:49.282
but he had that call

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:51.551
that he sold nearly $3.6 million in stock

00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:52.352
less than two weeks ago.

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:53.753
That's obviously worth zero tonight.

00:05:54.787 --> 00:05:55.988
I know you don't necessarily know

00:05:55.988 --> 00:05:57.256
the circumstances of that.

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:57.957
And I'm not trying to say

00:05:57.957 --> 00:05:59.025
it was nefarious I'm just saying.

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:01.728
What's your reaction when you hear that?

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:03.830
Erin, my reaction is

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:06.599
that this is so much bigger than a bank.

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:10.737
I run a nationwide telehealth company.

00:06:10.737 --> 00:06:11.404
We serve,

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:12.171
you know, prenatal

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:13.773
and postpartum patients.

00:06:13.773 --> 00:06:15.541
I have friends in health care, tech

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:17.944
and financial tech and property tech,

00:06:17.944 --> 00:06:20.680
all of us see the impact on human life.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.415
And so for us,

00:06:22.415 --> 00:06:22.749
you know,

00:06:22.749 --> 00:06:24.450
call with the CEO of the bank

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.219
is not our first priority.

00:06:26.219 --> 00:06:27.687
Our first priority is securing

00:06:27.687 --> 00:06:28.588
those funds.

00:06:28.588 --> 00:06:29.756
And making sure we can meet

00:06:29.756 --> 00:06:31.591
payroll on the 15th.

00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:34.394
So, you know, the human impact of this,

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:35.395
I think, is something

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:36.763
I cannot underscore enough.

00:06:36.763 --> 00:06:37.764
And, you know,

00:06:37.764 --> 00:06:38.564
my heart goes out to

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:39.732
so many business owners,

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:41.801
not just startups, but small business,

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:43.002
agriculture,

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:44.137
all the kinds of companies

00:06:44.137 --> 00:06:45.238
that bank with us

00:06:45.505 --> 00:06:46.539
where their founders

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:48.641
and owners are struggling right now,

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:50.176
trying to make ends meet,

00:06:50.176 --> 00:06:51.744
trying to make sense of you fear.

00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:53.045
And what you see is,

00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:53.379
I mean, we're

00:06:53.379 --> 00:06:54.514
not even talking about other banks.

00:06:54.514 --> 00:06:56.048
We're talking about ASB itself,

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:57.016
but that the impacts

00:06:57.016 --> 00:06:58.084
of this are significant

00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:00.353
I mean, they will affect

00:07:00.353 --> 00:07:01.421
everyday Americans.

00:07:01.421 --> 00:07:03.556
They will not just affect CDOs like me.

00:07:03.556 --> 00:07:05.391
We have team members in Arkansas,

00:07:05.391 --> 00:07:06.192
North Carolina,

00:07:06.192 --> 00:07:08.294
Washington State, Minnesota,

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:10.163
those people would be affected, too,

00:07:10.163 --> 00:07:11.230
had we not gotten those

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:12.064
funds out in time.

